@node Building Differential Dataflows
@section Building a differential computation

Differential dataflow is meant to be usable without understanding how it
works underneath.  This section covers the mechanics of putting a
computation together, before the next surveys the operators in depth.

@subsection The timely skeleton

A differential computation is a timely computation with a convenient set
of operators layered on top, so it needs a timely skeleton first: an
execution, a worker, and a dataflow built inside it.  The skeleton is
exactly the timely driver of @ref{Running Timely Dataflows}:
@code{dtfl_execute} (or @code{dtfl_execute_directly}) runs the worker;
@code{dtfl_worker_dataflow} builds the dataflow; the build function
creates the inputs, assembles the operators, and the driver advances the
inputs and steps the worker.

@subsection Input collections

Every differential computation starts from a user-controlled input
collection, which you can think of as a multiset: a bag of records that
may contain duplicates.  A @code{dtfl_input_session} drives one
(@pxref{Differential Interactions}): create it over a record type and a
diff class, add it to the scope with
@code{dtfl_input_session_to_collection}, and the collection it returns is
where the operators attach.  Changes made to the session, inserts and
removes at an advancing epoch, flow into the computation.

@subsection Operators, in brief

The operators build collections from collections.
@code{dtfl_collection_map} transforms each record (also the database
"projection": keep only the fields a later step needs, as early as
possible).  @code{dtfl_collection_filter} keeps the records a predicate
accepts; a @code{count} followed by a @code{filter} on the count can
change far less often than its input, since it reports only when a
count crosses the threshold.  @code{dtfl_collection_concat} merges two
collections, adding the multiplicities of each record.  The next section
covers these and the rest in full (@pxref{Differential Operators}).

@subsection Iteration, in brief

The signature feature is iteration.  @code{dtfl_collection_iterate}
takes a collection and a closure describing one step, and applies the
step to a fixed point.  To compute the transitive management relation,
the step joins the relation with itself to add one hop:

@example
/* if x manages y and y manages z, then x manages z */
swap  = dtfl_collection_map(manages, pair, swap_pair, DTFL_NOCTX);
hop   = dtfl_collection_join_map(swap, manages, u64, u64, u64,
            pair, drop_middle, DTFL_NOCTX);
return dtfl_collection_concat(hop, manages);   /* then distinct */
@end example

For iteration with several inputs, several loop variables, or several
outputs, build the iterative scope by hand: @code{dtfl_scope_iterative}
creates a subscope whose time adds a @code{u64} round counter,
@code{dtfl_collection_enter} brings an outer collection in, a
@code{dtfl_variable} carries each recursively defined collection, and
@code{dtfl_collection_leave} returns the result
(@pxref{Differential Operators}).
